# NotebookLM → Design Examples Workflow

When the user provides a JIRA story ID and/or a business use case name (e.g., `notebooklm NGASIM-3336` or `notebooklm "SMS Double Opt-In" NGASIM-3336`), execute the full workflow below automatically without asking for extra input unless a notebook cannot be unambiguously identified.

---

## Step 1 — Identify the Target Notebook

Call `notebook_list` (NotebookLM MCP) to retrieve all available notebooks.

Match the correct notebook using this priority order:
1. Exact or partial match of notebook title against the business use case name provided by the user
2. Partial match of notebook title against the JIRA story ID (e.g., `NGASIM-3336`)
3. If only one notebook exists, use it directly

If two or more notebooks remain as candidates after matching, present a numbered short list to the user and ask them to confirm with a single reply — do not proceed until confirmed.

---

## Step 2 — Get Notebook Details, Sources & Studio Notes (run in parallel)

Issue all three calls simultaneously once the notebook ID is known:

**2a. `notebook_get(notebook_id)`** — returns notebook metadata and the full list of sources with their IDs. Record every `source_id` for use in Steps 4 and 6.

**2b. `note_list(notebook_id)`** — fetches all Studio Notes from the notebook's Studio panel. These are user-authored notes such as:
- Sequence diagrams (mermaid syntax)
- Architecture sketches
- Decision logs
- Integration flow descriptions

Capture every note's title and full body content verbatim. Mermaid code blocks and any diagram syntax must be preserved exactly as-is — do not summarise, reformat, or truncate.

**2c. `notebook_describe(notebook_id)`** — returns an AI-generated notebook overview and suggested topics. Use this as the high-level summary seed for the output file header.

---

## Step 3 — Extract Design Content via notebook_query (run in parallel)

Run all five queries simultaneously against the notebook. If the notebook has 50 or more sources, use `notebook_query_start` for each query and poll `notebook_query_status` every 5 seconds until `status: completed` before proceeding to Step 4.

| Query ID | Question to send |
|---|---|
| Q1 | "Summarize the business use case and integration purpose in 3–5 sentences" |
| Q2 | "List all integration endpoints, downstream systems, data flows, and field mappings with types" |
| Q3 | "List all business rules, field validations, error handling requirements, and edge cases" |
| Q4 | "List every Jira story ID and every Confluence page URL referenced anywhere in the sources" |
| Q5 | "Produce a briefing document covering: key architectural decisions, open questions, risks, and the recommended implementation approach" |

Collect the full response text for each query. Do not summarise or truncate the answers.

---

## Step 4 — Extract AI Summaries (run in parallel with Step 3 where possible)

| Call | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `notebook_query`: "Generate a study guide with key concepts, definitions, acronyms, and open questions" | Study guide section |
| `notebook_query`: "Generate a FAQ — list the most important questions a developer would ask and answer each one" | FAQ section |
| `source_describe(source_id)` for each source ID from Step 2a | Per-source keyword chips and summary paragraph |

---

## Step 5 — Resolve Confluence Links

Scan all text collected in Steps 3 and 4 for Confluence URLs matching either pattern:
```
https://insulet.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
https://confluence.prod.insulet.com/wiki/spaces/{SPACE}/pages/{PAGE-ID}/{SLUG}
```

For each unique Confluence URL found:
1. Extract the numeric `{PAGE-ID}` from the URL
2. Call `confluence_get_page(page_id, include_body=true)` via the **Atlassian MCP** (`user-atlassian-guardrails`)
3. Strip any HTML markup from the returned body text
4. Record the page title, URL, and full plain-text body for the `## Confluence Pages` section

If no Confluence URLs are found, omit the `## Confluence Pages` section from the output file entirely.

---

## Step 6 — Raw Source Content Capture

For each `source_id` from Step 2a, call `source_get_content(source_id)`. This returns the verbatim indexed text of the source (PDF, web page, pasted text, YouTube transcript, etc.) with no AI processing. Append all results to the `## Raw Source Content` section, one subsection per source.

---

## Step 7 — Derive Output File Name

Apply the following rules in order:

```
businessUseCase = kebab-case slug derived from the notebook title or use case name
                  e.g. "SMS Double Opt-In"  →  sms-double-opt-in
                  e.g. "HCP Lead Creation"  →  hcp-lead-creation

storyId         = the JIRA ID supplied by the user
                  e.g. NGASIM-3336

filename        = {businessUseCase}_{storyId}.md
                  e.g. sms-double-opt-in_NGASIM-3336.md
```

If no JIRA story ID was provided by the user, omit the `_{storyId}` suffix and use just `{businessUseCase}.md`.

---

## Step 8 — Write Output File

Write the complete file to `agents/design/examples/{filename}`. Overwrite any existing file with the same name.

Use the following structure exactly. Omit any section whose content is entirely empty.

```markdown
# Design: {Human-Readable Use Case Name} — {STORY-ID}
Notebook: {notebook title}
Extracted: {YYYY-MM-DD}

---

## Business Use Case
{Q1 response}

---

## Integration Pattern & Data Flow
{Q2 response — endpoints, systems, flows, field mappings}

---

## Downstream Systems
{extracted from Q2 — list each downstream system with its role}

---

## Business Rules & Validations
{Q3 response — rules, validations, edge cases}

---

## Error Handling
{Q3 response — error scenarios and handling strategy}

---

## Briefing Document
{Q5 response — key decisions, open questions, risks, recommended approach}

---

## Study Guide
{study guide query response — key concepts, definitions, open questions}

---

## FAQ
{FAQ query response — developer questions and answers}

---

## Studio Notes
{for each note returned by note_list — one subsection per note}

### Note: {note title}
{verbatim note body — preserve all mermaid code blocks, diagram syntax, and formatting exactly as returned}

---

## Jira / Confluence References
{Q4 response — every Jira story ID and Confluence URL found}

---

## Confluence Pages
{only present if Confluence URLs were found and fetched in Step 5}

### Page: {page title} ({page ID})
Source: {URL}

{full plain-text body from confluence_get_page}

---

## Source Summaries
{for each source — title, keyword chips, and summary from source_describe}

### Source: {source title}
{source_describe output}

---

## Raw Source Content
{for each source — verbatim text from source_get_content}

### Source: {source title}
{verbatim source body}
```

---

## Step 9 — Confirm Completion

After writing the file, report:
- The full path of the file written: `agents/design/examples/{filename}`
- Number of Studio Notes captured
- Number of Confluence pages fetched
- Number of raw sources included
- A one-sentence description of the business use case extracted

> This file is now ready for use by `/use-raml`, `/use-develop`, and `/use-design` agents.
> Reference it via `@agents/design/examples/{filename}` in those agent chats.
